  "text": "The old lighthouse keeper, Silas, had seen more sunsets than most men. Eighty-seven years worth, to be precise, judging by the mournful creaks of his spine and the way his fingertips curled into a precarious, dry arrangement. He'd inherited the post from his father, who'd inherited it from *his* father. Generations of Silases had tended the light, a lonely sentinel against the crashing waves of the Pacific. He didn't mind the solitude. The ocean had always been his confidante, its moods reflecting his own \u2013 calm in the moments of peaceful contemplation, wild and furious during a storm.\n\nHe\u2019d spent countless nights tracing the constellations, not out of scientific curiosity, but out of a deep yearning for something\u2026 beyond. Not beyond the sea, he knew that well. Beyond the observable, the tangible. Beyond the relentless cycle of duty and disappointment. He\u2019d watch the patterns shift, born and die in shimmering glory, convinced there was a narrative written in light years, a story unfolding far beyond his grasp.\n\nHis granddaughter, Elvira, arrived a week ago. A whirlwind of bright colors and restless energy, she\u2019d announced her intention to learn the ropes, to experience \u2018real life\u2019 before succumbing to the monotony of academia. Silas didn't argue. He rarely did. He just pointed her towards the foghorn and tasked her with calibrating the reeds. The sea demanded respect, a need for precision, not fanciful notions.\n\nBut Elvira kept asking questions. Not about the workings of the light, but about the expanse above. About the pinches of loneliness that clung to the air like sea dust. About the shapes he saw when he focused his gaze upward, beyond the reach of his nimble fingers. He\u2019\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41/Grande building was beginning to suffer due to lack of care. He found he was remarkably vulnerable to her insistent curiosity. \n\nShe\u2019d spend hours on the balcony, a small frown creasing his otherwise jovial countenance. She brought with her a collection of strange instruments, complex circles of metal and focusing mechanisms, gleaming under the lamplight. He watched her adjust them, methodically, patiently, absorbing the movements of the air with practiced hands. \n\nThe first time he noticed a difference was subtle, a shimmer on the surface of the instruments. Then, a broader expansion of things \u2013 the way a certain vibrate of his finger brushes can be used to produce a tiny squeak, or the way a particular hue of color alighted on his face when he felt the unfortunate feeling of having to sit down. It was deeper than that, wasn't that? A whole orchestra of feeling appeared. \n\nHe looked out, past the observation area, and saw the world reflected in them. Not just the moon, the shifting expanse of water and the occasional glocktine of light. Not just the world he always knew, but\u2026 *it*. He didn\u2019s let out my feelings \u2013 \u201cOh! That is a delightful, melancholic sigh I would say, it sounds like it is ready to cry!\u201d His inward voice. It was dark for Elvira. \u201cDon\u2019t mind me,\u201d he sighed quietly, \u201cI\u2019m merely awkwardly clearing my alistines.\u201d\n\nHe didn't need to be told, but he asked, \"What do you hear?\u201d \nElvira only smiled. \"You, Silas,\" she said, \"are seeing everywhere.\"",
